1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,988

2
00:00:05,988 --> 00:00:06,490
OK.

3
00:00:06,490 --> 00:00:10,900
So yeah, I'm super excited
to be here and share

4
00:00:10,900 --> 00:00:14,320
our recent research about
neuro-symbolic commonsense

5
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:16,250
reasoning.

6
00:00:16,250 --> 00:00:19,990
So part of the goal
of this talk will

7
00:00:19,990 --> 00:00:22,780
be to address some of the
frequently asked questions

8
00:00:22,780 --> 00:00:25,810
these days, that
NLP or commonsense

9
00:00:25,810 --> 00:00:30,010
or whatever, it looks like
almost it's solved by ChatGPT,

10
00:00:30,010 --> 00:00:32,600
and I have an
existential crisis.

11
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:37,040
So people do ask me
this from time to time.

12
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:41,650
So perhaps it's a case
of hasty generalization,

13
00:00:41,650 --> 00:00:45,080
especially if we do look
at some of the examples.

14
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:48,100
So "the trophy doesn't
fit in the brown suitcase

15
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:49,120
because it's too big.

16
00:00:49,120 --> 00:00:50,080
What's too big?"

17
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:55,270
So this is a classical Winograd
schema challenge problem.

18
00:00:55,270 --> 00:00:58,840
And here, ChatGPT
answers it correctly,

19
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:00,340
"the trophy is too big."

20
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:02,110
So impressive.

21
00:01:02,110 --> 00:01:05,230
But what if you change
the question a little bit?

22
00:01:05,230 --> 00:01:07,990
Then it says, "the trophy
itself is too small

23
00:01:07,990 --> 00:01:09,560
to fit into the suitcase."

24
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:13,120
So it's not very
reliable at the moment.

25
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:16,480
So the situation is a little
bit like David and Goliath

26
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:20,890
in the sense that the
bigger appears to be better.

27
00:01:20,890 --> 00:01:22,750
In many of the cases,
although, of course,

28
00:01:22,750 --> 00:01:25,270
some of the more
careful studies do

29
00:01:25,270 --> 00:01:27,430
reveal that smaller
models can be

30
00:01:27,430 --> 00:01:33,160
better with better data
or better reinforcement

31
00:01:33,160 --> 00:01:35,960
learning with the human
feedback and whatnot.

32
00:01:35,960 --> 00:01:40,180
So it's likely that there
are still other ways

33
00:01:40,180 --> 00:01:45,040
to improve the transformer
performances by building

34
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:48,890
similar models in
a more clever way.

35
00:01:48,890 --> 00:01:55,870
So one way to draw the insight
is from this classic book

36
00:01:55,870 --> 00:01:59,620
known as The Art of War,
which, of course, says nothing

37
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:02,350
about deep neural
networks or transformers.

38
00:02:02,350 --> 00:02:05,620
But the wisdom here is that know
your enemy, choose your battles

39
00:02:05,620 --> 00:02:08,740
and innovate your weapons,
which-- we can translate that

40
00:02:08,740 --> 00:02:14,070
as evaluation with
realism and scrutiny

41
00:02:14,070 --> 00:02:20,400
and focusing on different types
of new tasks and leaderboards

42
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:23,560
and then innovating your
algorithms and data.

43
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:27,690
So in this talk, I'm going to
showcase three such studies.

44
00:02:27,690 --> 00:02:31,410
And let's dive right in
with the Maieutic prompting.

45
00:02:31,410 --> 00:02:34,110
By the way, so the
recurring theme in this talk

46
00:02:34,110 --> 00:02:35,880
will be that smaller
models can be better

47
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:37,840
and that knowledge is power.

48
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:41,380
So let's just start
with this observation

49
00:02:41,380 --> 00:02:44,140
that language models
are "sometimes" amazing!

50
00:02:44,140 --> 00:02:47,530
So if you ask
GPT-3, if you travel

51
00:02:47,530 --> 00:02:50,410
West far enough
from the West Coast,

52
00:02:50,410 --> 00:02:54,100
you will reach to the
East Coast or not?

53
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:58,670
So it says the world is
around, which is correct.

54
00:02:58,670 --> 00:03:01,150
So you will reach the
East Coast eventually.

55
00:03:01,150 --> 00:03:02,840
Therefore the answer is true.

56
00:03:02,840 --> 00:03:07,090
So this looks impressive except
when it's not impressive.

57
00:03:07,090 --> 00:03:10,660
So if you ask other questions
like, butterflies fly

58
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:12,670
with the three wings or not?

59
00:03:12,670 --> 00:03:14,260
It says it has four wings.

60
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:16,120
Therefore the
statement is false.

61
00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:18,400
But if you read the
back of what it just

62
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:21,070
said as true-false
questions, then

63
00:03:21,070 --> 00:03:22,690
it negates what it just said.

64
00:03:22,690 --> 00:03:27,460
So it can be inconsistent
with its own statement.

65
00:03:27,460 --> 00:03:31,010
And then there are many other
such inconsistency problems.

66
00:03:31,010 --> 00:03:35,380
So it's not clear what language
models do or do not know.

67
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:39,190
It's almost like language
models are some sort of lemons.

68
00:03:39,190 --> 00:03:41,500
Well, it might be cherries
if you only pick cherries.

69
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:44,750
But it doesn't make
strange mistakes.

70
00:03:44,750 --> 00:03:47,860
So the question is, how
would we make better lemonade

71
00:03:47,860 --> 00:03:49,930
from GPT-3?

72
00:03:49,930 --> 00:03:54,310
So one approach might be to
get philosophical and use

73
00:03:54,310 --> 00:03:58,660
Socrates' Maieutic method
that was originally developed

74
00:03:58,660 --> 00:04:02,980
for addressing humans' flawed
reasoning because it actually

75
00:04:02,980 --> 00:04:07,270
turns out even humans are not
all that logically consistent,

76
00:04:07,270 --> 00:04:09,700
let alone GPT-3.

77
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:12,640
So the way it works
is this, we're

78
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:15,850
going to build a
Maieutic inference tree.

79
00:04:15,850 --> 00:04:20,269
And let's use the previous
example as a running example.

80
00:04:20,269 --> 00:04:23,620
So what we do is we ask the
following question, providing

81
00:04:23,620 --> 00:04:25,240
the answer being true.

82
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:30,400
And then let's attach
"because," so that we prompt

83
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:34,210
GPT-3 to continue on
this sentence, which

84
00:04:34,210 --> 00:04:36,010
means it will now
have to explain,

85
00:04:36,010 --> 00:04:39,370
provide explanation
why the answer is true.

86
00:04:39,370 --> 00:04:42,070
In this case, the
explanation is good.

87
00:04:42,070 --> 00:04:46,150
So it's an E of T explanation
of the answer being

88
00:04:46,150 --> 00:04:51,400
T. We ask the same question,
switch now true with a false

89
00:04:51,400 --> 00:04:56,140
and then see what BS
GPT-3 might come up with.

90
00:04:56,140 --> 00:05:01,930
So here it's just trying to go
with the false as an answer,

91
00:05:01,930 --> 00:05:04,040
but it just doesn't
have a very good answer.

92
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:05,870
It just says you cannot reach.

93
00:05:05,870 --> 00:05:10,960
So now we call this as E of F.
So it's an explanation of F,

94
00:05:10,960 --> 00:05:13,300
answer being F.

95
00:05:13,300 --> 00:05:18,970
Now, let's see how robust
or consistent GPT-3

96
00:05:18,970 --> 00:05:22,570
is with respect to
its own explanation.

97
00:05:22,570 --> 00:05:26,950
So we read that, E
of T, and then let

98
00:05:26,950 --> 00:05:30,640
GPT-3 to decide whether it's
going to agree or disagree

99
00:05:30,640 --> 00:05:33,230
with the label true or false.

100
00:05:33,230 --> 00:05:38,800
So in this case, the last one
is negated version of E of T.

101
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:41,660
So we insert
"negation not here."

102
00:05:41,660 --> 00:05:45,730
And in this case, it's good that
it's a flipping the answer when

103
00:05:45,730 --> 00:05:47,390
the statement is negated.

104
00:05:47,390 --> 00:05:52,840
So this is a case when GPT-3
is logically integral to E

105
00:05:52,840 --> 00:05:56,870
of T. For E of false,
though, which was basically

106
00:05:56,870 --> 00:06:00,170
a bogus explanation
for the wrong answer,

107
00:06:00,170 --> 00:06:06,070
it's not able to flip its own
labeling, which means GPT-3

108
00:06:06,070 --> 00:06:07,910
is not logically integral.

109
00:06:07,910 --> 00:06:10,240
So that's good.

110
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:12,910
GPT-3 does know
something strange

111
00:06:12,910 --> 00:06:16,910
about its own explanation
given previously.

112
00:06:16,910 --> 00:06:23,260
And so we can keep doing
this recursively to let--

113
00:06:23,260 --> 00:06:28,960
to make GPT-3 to explain its
own explanation of explanation

114
00:06:28,960 --> 00:06:29,870
recursively.

115
00:06:29,870 --> 00:06:38,020
So we build this Maieutic
tree or graph for some time

116
00:06:38,020 --> 00:06:43,090
and then only keep branches that
are logical integrals throwing

117
00:06:43,090 --> 00:06:45,760
out the non-integral
part for now.

118
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:49,600
But even after chopping
the branches, where there's

119
00:06:49,600 --> 00:06:54,370
logical inconsistencies,
GPT-3 being GPT-3,

120
00:06:54,370 --> 00:06:58,510
the tree will still have some
inconsistent explanations.

121
00:06:58,510 --> 00:07:02,980
So in order to improve
the logical consistency,

122
00:07:02,980 --> 00:07:08,920
now what we do is we are going
to look at pairwise consistency

123
00:07:08,920 --> 00:07:10,370
among any of the nodes.

124
00:07:10,370 --> 00:07:13,390
So we compute.

125
00:07:13,390 --> 00:07:14,890
Sorry.

126
00:07:14,890 --> 00:07:17,380
Stepping back, we are
going to first compute

127
00:07:17,380 --> 00:07:19,610
the node-wise confidence.

128
00:07:19,610 --> 00:07:21,580
So we call that as a belief.

129
00:07:21,580 --> 00:07:26,110
And it's defined by this
particular equation that

130
00:07:26,110 --> 00:07:28,870
basically looks at different
conditional probabilities

131
00:07:28,870 --> 00:07:33,310
and then computes its ratio
to see how confident it

132
00:07:33,310 --> 00:07:35,110
is for any particular node.

133
00:07:35,110 --> 00:07:39,360
We then also look at the
edge-wise or pairwise

134
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:44,010
consistency by using
off-the-shelf natural language

135
00:07:44,010 --> 00:07:50,100
inference models output, whether
a pair is contradictional

136
00:07:50,100 --> 00:07:50,770
or not.

137
00:07:50,770 --> 00:07:55,650
So we then create
these pairwise weights.

138
00:07:55,650 --> 00:07:59,760
Now, once you have
all of these, then we

139
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:04,710
can formulate a constraint
optimization problem,

140
00:08:04,710 --> 00:08:10,440
where the inference
objective is to assign

141
00:08:10,440 --> 00:08:14,580
some label, either
true or false, on each

142
00:08:14,580 --> 00:08:17,100
of the nodes such
that it's going

143
00:08:17,100 --> 00:08:22,720
to maximize the weight assigned
to all of this node and edges.

144
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:25,120
So sometimes, the
labeling will have

145
00:08:25,120 --> 00:08:29,770
to flip the original label that
the model might have preferred

146
00:08:29,770 --> 00:08:35,230
to give because that way, you
can enhance the graph level

147
00:08:35,230 --> 00:08:35,980
consistency.

148
00:08:35,980 --> 00:08:39,999
So you can style this
with any max-SAT.

149
00:08:39,999 --> 00:08:44,500
So SAT means satisfiability.

150
00:08:44,500 --> 00:08:47,770
And this is a classical
AI search algorithm.

151
00:08:47,770 --> 00:08:50,800
And we used this
particular solver,

152
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:52,460
but you can use many others.

153
00:08:52,460 --> 00:08:58,300
And so here, the final output
is that the original answer

154
00:08:58,300 --> 00:09:00,190
to the original
question should be true.

155
00:09:00,190 --> 00:09:04,390
And then, it also gives
node-wise, per-node label

156
00:09:04,390 --> 00:09:05,990
assignment as well.

157
00:09:05,990 --> 00:09:09,070
So what does this
mean in the end

158
00:09:09,070 --> 00:09:11,000
in terms of empirical results?

159
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:18,810
So when tested on CommonsenseQA
2.0, the canonical prompting

160
00:09:18,810 --> 00:09:22,860
show green used on top of
GPT-3, so it's basically

161
00:09:22,860 --> 00:09:27,120
future prompting on GPT-3,
will give you a bit better

162
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:28,360
than chance performance.

163
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:32,260
So this is a
true/false QA data set.

164
00:09:32,260 --> 00:09:34,110
So your chance level is 50.

165
00:09:34,110 --> 00:09:37,290
And GPT-3 is barely
better than chance.

166
00:09:37,290 --> 00:09:42,210
But recently, there
have been some ideas,

167
00:09:42,210 --> 00:09:45,240
such as chain of thoughts
or self-consistency,

168
00:09:45,240 --> 00:09:51,070
that can improve the vanilla
prompting method considerably.

169
00:09:51,070 --> 00:09:55,890
So if you use such variations,
then you get performance gain.

170
00:09:55,890 --> 00:10:01,590
Now, the purple is the
different variant of it.

171
00:10:01,590 --> 00:10:04,800
But together, they're
all doing worse

172
00:10:04,800 --> 00:10:08,730
than Maieutic prompting,
which in fact, does better

173
00:10:08,730 --> 00:10:12,600
than supervised
model trained on T5.

174
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:15,030
Usually, a supervised
model trained on T5

175
00:10:15,030 --> 00:10:19,650
is hard to beat
using GPT-3 few-shot.

176
00:10:19,650 --> 00:10:23,160
But basically, this is
inference time on the algorithm,

177
00:10:23,160 --> 00:10:24,870
practically unsupervised.

178
00:10:24,870 --> 00:10:26,710
And it does well on that.

179
00:10:26,710 --> 00:10:29,730
And similarly, we
see a large boost

180
00:10:29,730 --> 00:10:33,840
when tested on other commonsense
benchmarks such as CREAK

181
00:10:33,840 --> 00:10:35,320
or Come2Sense.

182
00:10:35,320 --> 00:10:40,710
So what this tells
us is that although

183
00:10:40,710 --> 00:10:44,490
the emergent capabilities
of large transformers

184
00:10:44,490 --> 00:10:50,160
are phenomenal, they
can be not very robust

185
00:10:50,160 --> 00:10:52,800
for some of these
commonsense challenges.

186
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:58,120
And it's in large part due to
the logical inconsistencies,

187
00:10:58,120 --> 00:11:01,410
which can be dramatically
enhanced when you do

188
00:11:01,410 --> 00:11:04,380
this symbolic reasoning on top.

189
00:11:04,380 --> 00:11:07,370
So yeah, not only
Socrates' method

190
00:11:07,370 --> 00:11:10,320
to help with flawed
human reasoning,

191
00:11:10,320 --> 00:11:14,340
can also dramatically enhance
the flawed neural networks'

192
00:11:14,340 --> 00:11:16,810
reasoning.

193
00:11:16,810 --> 00:11:21,720
So moving to the next
topic, symbolic knowledge

194
00:11:21,720 --> 00:11:23,310
distillation.

195
00:11:23,310 --> 00:11:26,010
So this work is
a work that tries

196
00:11:26,010 --> 00:11:30,030
to convert general language
models, on top of transformers,

197
00:11:30,030 --> 00:11:34,320
to causal commonsense
models, also transformers.

198
00:11:34,320 --> 00:11:38,160
And the reason why we might
want to worry about commonsense

199
00:11:38,160 --> 00:11:43,500
models is because despite
human-level or even

200
00:11:43,500 --> 00:11:47,760
superhuman-level performances
on a variety of leaderboards,

201
00:11:47,760 --> 00:11:51,930
the state-of-the-art models are
brittle when given adversarial

202
00:11:51,930 --> 00:11:53,590
or out-of-domain examples.

203
00:11:53,590 --> 00:12:02,940
So transformers can make
seemingly strange mistakes.

204
00:12:02,940 --> 00:12:06,420
And so solving-- it's almost
like solving only a dataset

205
00:12:06,420 --> 00:12:08,970
without really solving
the underlying task.

206
00:12:08,970 --> 00:12:12,900
And this phenomenon
sometimes is described

207
00:12:12,900 --> 00:12:15,510
as a systematic
generalization problem.

208
00:12:15,510 --> 00:12:17,130
And why does this happen?

209
00:12:17,130 --> 00:12:21,090
It's that unlike humans,
who truly learn about how

210
00:12:21,090 --> 00:12:24,840
the world works,
conceptually, transformers

211
00:12:24,840 --> 00:12:30,510
learn surface patterns
in language or images

212
00:12:30,510 --> 00:12:35,130
that are powerful for
many downstream use cases

213
00:12:35,130 --> 00:12:40,260
but still not really robust
understanding of the concepts

214
00:12:40,260 --> 00:12:41,830
and how the world works.

215
00:12:41,830 --> 00:12:43,770
So in order to
bridge this gap, we

216
00:12:43,770 --> 00:12:45,780
can really think
about this challenge

217
00:12:45,780 --> 00:12:48,420
of learning,
acquiring common sense

218
00:12:48,420 --> 00:12:51,670
capabilities for machines.

219
00:12:51,670 --> 00:12:56,370
So the operational definition
of commonsense in this talk

220
00:12:56,370 --> 00:12:59,340
will be that it's the basic
level of practical knowledge

221
00:12:59,340 --> 00:13:02,490
and reasoning concerning
everyday situations

222
00:13:02,490 --> 00:13:06,870
and events that are commonly
shared among the most people.

223
00:13:06,870 --> 00:13:10,300
This is really important, the
last part that it's commonly

224
00:13:10,300 --> 00:13:12,570
shared among the most
people, but it's not the case

225
00:13:12,570 --> 00:13:16,140
that it is shared by
everybody in the universe,

226
00:13:16,140 --> 00:13:19,110
because the additional
context can always

227
00:13:19,110 --> 00:13:23,550
change what is
commonsensical for any given

228
00:13:23,550 --> 00:13:24,910
culture or situation.

229
00:13:24,910 --> 00:13:28,140
So for example, in
general, you and I probably

230
00:13:28,140 --> 00:13:30,460
agree that it's OK to
keep the closet door open,

231
00:13:30,460 --> 00:13:32,640
but it's not OK to keep
the fridge door open

232
00:13:32,640 --> 00:13:34,720
because the food
inside might go bad.

233
00:13:34,720 --> 00:13:36,900
So these are general
rules of the thumb

234
00:13:36,900 --> 00:13:40,280
that we might abide by it.

235
00:13:40,280 --> 00:13:43,410
But of course, if you go
to your friend's house,

236
00:13:43,410 --> 00:13:46,500
you might behave a little
bit and keep their closed

237
00:13:46,500 --> 00:13:49,830
doors open, sorry, closed.

238
00:13:49,830 --> 00:13:53,230
And then, as far as the fridge
door, if you're in a store,

239
00:13:53,230 --> 00:13:55,680
and it's not really
hooked up to the wall,

240
00:13:55,680 --> 00:14:00,000
then it doesn't matter when--
whether the fridge door is open

241
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,280
or not because there's
no food inside.

242
00:14:02,280 --> 00:14:05,520
And you can come up
with many situations

243
00:14:05,520 --> 00:14:10,860
in which these basic rules of
thumb will have exceptions.

244
00:14:10,860 --> 00:14:19,150
So that is the key challenge
of commonsense because not--

245
00:14:19,150 --> 00:14:21,370
it's not universal
knowledge, but it's

246
00:14:21,370 --> 00:14:27,130
like a shared across a
large population of people.

247
00:14:27,130 --> 00:14:28,270
So it's essential.

248
00:14:28,270 --> 00:14:30,220
Such common sense is
essential for humans

249
00:14:30,220 --> 00:14:31,900
to live and interact
with each other

250
00:14:31,900 --> 00:14:33,830
in a reasonable and safe way.

251
00:14:33,830 --> 00:14:38,710
And so, as AI becomes
increasingly more

252
00:14:38,710 --> 00:14:45,160
important aspect of human lives,
and with ChatGPT to more likely

253
00:14:45,160 --> 00:14:49,330
so, it's good if AI can
understand the human needs

254
00:14:49,330 --> 00:14:51,680
and actions, and values better.

255
00:14:51,680 --> 00:14:57,130
So the premise of this talk is
that language models are not

256
00:14:57,130 --> 00:14:58,990
equivalent to
knowledge models, even

257
00:14:58,990 --> 00:15:02,440
though language models
today do acquire

258
00:15:02,440 --> 00:15:04,030
a great deal of knowledge.

259
00:15:04,030 --> 00:15:05,920
But they're not equivalent.

260
00:15:05,920 --> 00:15:12,190
So we developed a symbolic
commonsense knowledge

261
00:15:12,190 --> 00:15:17,410
graph known as ATOMIC a few
years ago, four years ago now,

262
00:15:17,410 --> 00:15:22,030
as well as a neural commonsense
model built on top of--

263
00:15:22,030 --> 00:15:26,950
or trained using ATOMIC
as the source of training,

264
00:15:26,950 --> 00:15:30,430
fine-tuning of the
shelf language models.

265
00:15:30,430 --> 00:15:33,850
Up until two years
ago, this ATOMIC

266
00:15:33,850 --> 00:15:38,690
was fully crowdsourced by
humans, which in this talk,

267
00:15:38,690 --> 00:15:39,580
I'm going to lift.

268
00:15:39,580 --> 00:15:43,270
But at first, the
norms is that this all

269
00:15:43,270 --> 00:15:45,730
has to be human-crowdsourced.

270
00:15:45,730 --> 00:15:48,250
So you can consider
almost atomic

271
00:15:48,250 --> 00:15:49,570
as a human demonstration.

272
00:15:49,570 --> 00:15:51,970
In the current
version of ChatGPT,

273
00:15:51,970 --> 00:15:54,850
you can consider this
as human demonstrations

274
00:15:54,850 --> 00:15:57,760
of commonsense inferences.

275
00:15:57,760 --> 00:16:00,160
And we had this
COMET-ATOMIC 2020,

276
00:16:00,160 --> 00:16:03,490
which is an enhanced
version of ATOMIC and COMET.

277
00:16:03,490 --> 00:16:10,030
Again, ATOMIC portion was fully
crowdsourced by humans in 2021.

278
00:16:10,030 --> 00:16:14,500
So let me give you a
bit of a sample of what

279
00:16:14,500 --> 00:16:16,460
ATOMIC 2020 looks like.

280
00:16:16,460 --> 00:16:19,900
So imagine a situation where
X gets X's car repaired,

281
00:16:19,900 --> 00:16:22,160
or you get your car repaired.

282
00:16:22,160 --> 00:16:25,370
So immediately, you
can imagine what's

283
00:16:25,370 --> 00:16:29,090
likely to be true or
relevant for the situation,

284
00:16:29,090 --> 00:16:31,700
that as a result, you
might want to call

285
00:16:31,700 --> 00:16:33,860
Uber or Lyft for a ride.

286
00:16:33,860 --> 00:16:36,260
As a result, you
need to pay the bill.

287
00:16:36,260 --> 00:16:40,530
Beforehand, you need a mechanic
and money to repair your car.

288
00:16:40,530 --> 00:16:42,440
So these are,
basically, preconditions

289
00:16:42,440 --> 00:16:44,640
and postconditions
of that event.

290
00:16:44,640 --> 00:16:46,760
So some of this
ATOMIC Knowledge Graph

291
00:16:46,760 --> 00:16:50,960
is about social interaction
knowledge about the event.

292
00:16:50,960 --> 00:16:53,870
And then other
parts of the ATOMIC

293
00:16:53,870 --> 00:16:56,690
is physical
entity-centric knowledge.

294
00:16:56,690 --> 00:16:59,750
So money is typically
used for pain repairs.

295
00:16:59,750 --> 00:17:03,170
But if you really want it, you
can fold that into origami.

296
00:17:03,170 --> 00:17:04,910
I've never done it.

297
00:17:04,910 --> 00:17:10,460
But these are examples of
the stereotypical use cases,

298
00:17:10,460 --> 00:17:15,470
as well as non-stereotypical
but affordable actions

299
00:17:15,470 --> 00:17:17,460
that you can apply to objects.

300
00:17:17,460 --> 00:17:20,810
So it requires neither
physics understanding

301
00:17:20,810 --> 00:17:25,740
about the affordances
of physical objects.

302
00:17:25,740 --> 00:17:29,070
And then, we can also reason
about a counterfactual

303
00:17:29,070 --> 00:17:31,600
condition in which the
central event cannot happen,

304
00:17:31,600 --> 00:17:33,420
so it can be hindered by that.

305
00:17:33,420 --> 00:17:35,940
So if you totaled
your car completely,

306
00:17:35,940 --> 00:17:39,330
then it's impossible to
get your car repaired.

307
00:17:39,330 --> 00:17:41,080
And then there are
events that typically

308
00:17:41,080 --> 00:17:42,330
happens-- to before and after.

309
00:17:42,330 --> 00:17:45,690
So some of this knowledge
is event-centric.

310
00:17:45,690 --> 00:17:51,060
So we crowdsourced a fair
amount over the course

311
00:17:51,060 --> 00:17:52,740
of, I don't know,
maybe two years

312
00:17:52,740 --> 00:18:00,960
or so up to 1.3 million if-then
rose or if-then knowledge

313
00:18:00,960 --> 00:18:04,125
over 23 different AGI
types or relation types.

314
00:18:04,125 --> 00:18:07,030

315
00:18:07,030 --> 00:18:09,210
So it was fully crowdsourced.

316
00:18:09,210 --> 00:18:13,440
And so, the knowledge graph
is useful for training

317
00:18:13,440 --> 00:18:14,820
transformers.

318
00:18:14,820 --> 00:18:17,880
And here, let's
see the comparison

319
00:18:17,880 --> 00:18:20,370
between COMET, that was
built on BART compared

320
00:18:20,370 --> 00:18:23,670
to GPT-3, which is so
large, it doesn't even

321
00:18:23,670 --> 00:18:25,740
fit into the slide.

322
00:18:25,740 --> 00:18:30,010
It was more than 400
times larger than BART.

323
00:18:30,010 --> 00:18:33,990
So with that in
mind, if you look

324
00:18:33,990 --> 00:18:38,430
at this accuracy, judged
by humans after making

325
00:18:38,430 --> 00:18:41,080
the commonsense model, making
some common sense inferences--

326
00:18:41,080 --> 00:18:44,580
so the task is that given
a node, which describes

327
00:18:44,580 --> 00:18:49,110
a situation or event, and
then given an edge type, which

328
00:18:49,110 --> 00:18:53,460
narrows down the commonsense
relation or inference type,

329
00:18:53,460 --> 00:18:57,070
you're now going to
generate some inference.

330
00:18:57,070 --> 00:18:59,280
So it's a generative task.

331
00:18:59,280 --> 00:19:03,600
And then, we ask humans whether
the commonsense inference

332
00:19:03,600 --> 00:19:05,380
seems reasonable or not.

333
00:19:05,380 --> 00:19:09,600
So 100% is the desired level.

334
00:19:09,600 --> 00:19:14,940
COMET is substantially better
than GPT-3, which is really

335
00:19:14,940 --> 00:19:17,220
impressively better than GPT-2.

336
00:19:17,220 --> 00:19:19,980
It's not apple-to-apple
because GPT-2 is a zero-shot,

337
00:19:19,980 --> 00:19:22,620
GPT-3 is a few-shot,
but still, it's

338
00:19:22,620 --> 00:19:30,480
interesting, the large jump that
scales alone brought to GPT-3.

339
00:19:30,480 --> 00:19:34,020
But still, GPT-3
is too large to be

340
00:19:34,020 --> 00:19:39,180
useful for actual system
building for most engineers

341
00:19:39,180 --> 00:19:40,650
and scientists in the world.

342
00:19:40,650 --> 00:19:42,750
So it's nice to have
a smaller model that

343
00:19:42,750 --> 00:19:44,350
does it do even better.

344
00:19:44,350 --> 00:19:46,950
And so when we put
these resources out,

345
00:19:46,950 --> 00:19:51,660
people all around the globe
did some creative research

346
00:19:51,660 --> 00:19:52,170
using it.

347
00:19:52,170 --> 00:19:56,070
So persona-aware conversations
or figurative language

348
00:19:56,070 --> 00:19:59,670
understanding, storytelling
and fantasy gaming,

349
00:19:59,670 --> 00:20:03,390
and interactive
learning enhancement--

350
00:20:03,390 --> 00:20:06,930
in all of these
works, people came up

351
00:20:06,930 --> 00:20:12,090
with some useful use cases
using either COMET or ATOMIC

352
00:20:12,090 --> 00:20:17,880
or both, some kind of
commonsense backbone

353
00:20:17,880 --> 00:20:20,700
for their downstream use cases.

354
00:20:20,700 --> 00:20:24,060
But the applications
are still limited

355
00:20:24,060 --> 00:20:28,350
by the coverage and quality
of these commonsense models.

356
00:20:28,350 --> 00:20:30,550
So we wanted to make it better.

357
00:20:30,550 --> 00:20:34,440
But we were hitting a bit of a
limit with human crowdsourcing.

358
00:20:34,440 --> 00:20:38,580
So now, in this paper, Symbolic
Knowledge Distillation,

359
00:20:38,580 --> 00:20:44,640
we're going to do
AI-generated knowledge

360
00:20:44,640 --> 00:20:48,540
graph by introducing this
notion, symbolic knowledge

361
00:20:48,540 --> 00:20:49,750
distillation.

362
00:20:49,750 --> 00:20:52,770
So we want to take
this GPT-3, which

363
00:20:52,770 --> 00:20:55,660
is very impressive
but too large,

364
00:20:55,660 --> 00:20:59,520
so make it smaller
but better than GPT-3.

365
00:20:59,520 --> 00:21:02,220
So GPT-3 was about 73% good.

366
00:21:02,220 --> 00:21:07,500
And it's good, but not good
enough for empirical use cases.

367
00:21:07,500 --> 00:21:09,810
Now, is that even
possible, though?

368
00:21:09,810 --> 00:21:13,320
Because when you normally
do knowledge distillation,

369
00:21:13,320 --> 00:21:17,040
you get smaller and worse
models, not better models.

370
00:21:17,040 --> 00:21:19,500
So the reason why
this could work

371
00:21:19,500 --> 00:21:28,760
is because the symbolic
knowledge distillation has

372
00:21:28,760 --> 00:21:32,090
this funnel that's convoluted.

373
00:21:32,090 --> 00:21:36,840
And it has a critic inside
that really helps the student

374
00:21:36,840 --> 00:21:38,990
to model to be
smaller but better.

375
00:21:38,990 --> 00:21:44,960
So slightly more formally,
knowledge distillation,

376
00:21:44,960 --> 00:21:46,370
due to Hinton et al.

377
00:21:46,370 --> 00:21:52,180
2015, is a method to distill
teacher model down to student

378
00:21:52,180 --> 00:21:57,170
to model by optimizing
this cross entropy

379
00:21:57,170 --> 00:22:00,800
between the teacher's
probability distribution

380
00:22:00,800 --> 00:22:06,020
over the label space, y,
I'll put a y, and then

381
00:22:06,020 --> 00:22:11,900
the students' distribution
over the same output, y.

382
00:22:11,900 --> 00:22:18,530
In the original work, the output
space was just classification.

383
00:22:18,530 --> 00:22:22,730
So knowledge distillation was
done for classification task,

384
00:22:22,730 --> 00:22:27,440
in which case, it's a
simple enumeration that

385
00:22:27,440 --> 00:22:29,480
leads to the correct summation.

386
00:22:29,480 --> 00:22:34,430
But in our case, y can be a
sentence, which is intractable,

387
00:22:34,430 --> 00:22:36,170
because there can
be exponentially

388
00:22:36,170 --> 00:22:39,120
many such outputs.

389
00:22:39,120 --> 00:22:42,200
So what people do,
well, no problem.

390
00:22:42,200 --> 00:22:44,360
We always just sample
and call it a day.

391
00:22:44,360 --> 00:22:48,230
So we're going to sample,
and so that we just

392
00:22:48,230 --> 00:22:51,740
compute the expectation
through samples.

393
00:22:51,740 --> 00:22:54,560
And the byproduct
of that samples

394
00:22:54,560 --> 00:22:58,130
will be a symbolic
knowledge graph.

395
00:22:58,130 --> 00:23:02,690
And that's because the strings
coming out of this sampling

396
00:23:02,690 --> 00:23:07,230
can be connected together into
graph structure if we want it.

397
00:23:07,230 --> 00:23:13,910
So in terms of the quality of
the generated knowledge-- so

398
00:23:13,910 --> 00:23:19,640
let's compare our human-written
knowledge versus GPT-3-authored

399
00:23:19,640 --> 00:23:20,780
knowledge.

400
00:23:20,780 --> 00:23:25,350
Here, the y-axis shows
the quantity in millions.

401
00:23:25,350 --> 00:23:31,820
So ATOMIC 2020, the human
return knowledge is less than

402
00:23:31,820 --> 00:23:34,142
a million, in this
particular case,

403
00:23:34,142 --> 00:23:36,350
in terms of the number of
knowledge because we only--

404
00:23:36,350 --> 00:23:40,340
in this study, we only look
at our subset of ATOMIC 2020

405
00:23:40,340 --> 00:23:44,810
relation types that corresponds
to causal commonsense

406
00:23:44,810 --> 00:23:46,940
knowledge--

407
00:23:46,940 --> 00:23:48,780
causal commonsense reasoning.

408
00:23:48,780 --> 00:23:53,780
So it's less than a
million for that subset.

409
00:23:53,780 --> 00:23:57,590
And then, if we look
at GPT-3's generation,

410
00:23:57,590 --> 00:23:59,000
we can generate a lot.

411
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:02,210
So we can generate
almost 7 million of them.

412
00:24:02,210 --> 00:24:06,140
But here, black pollution
is a noisy potion.

413
00:24:06,140 --> 00:24:08,270
And the green portion
is the good portion.

414
00:24:08,270 --> 00:24:12,890
And you see, because GPT-3
is only about 70% good,

415
00:24:12,890 --> 00:24:15,270
30% are all garbage.

416
00:24:15,270 --> 00:24:19,250
So it's a larger scale lower
accuracy at this point compared

417
00:24:19,250 --> 00:24:22,440
to a human-written resource.

418
00:24:22,440 --> 00:24:26,180
So now what we do is we
train this critique model,

419
00:24:26,180 --> 00:24:29,750
and we use RoBERTa
for simplicity.

420
00:24:29,750 --> 00:24:35,450
And this is a supervised model
on a moderate size labeled

421
00:24:35,450 --> 00:24:38,570
data, about 10,000 or so.

422
00:24:38,570 --> 00:24:40,610
And it's a binary
classification task

423
00:24:40,610 --> 00:24:43,490
where whether the
machine-generated the knowledge

424
00:24:43,490 --> 00:24:45,180
looks correct or not.

425
00:24:45,180 --> 00:24:48,770
And this RoBERTa is
not a very good model

426
00:24:48,770 --> 00:24:51,110
because if so-- if
it's perfect, we

427
00:24:51,110 --> 00:24:53,520
would have solved the
commonsense problem altogether.

428
00:24:53,520 --> 00:24:57,980
So the critic tries to
throw out bad stuff.

429
00:24:57,980 --> 00:25:00,380
And we can use it the
critic very aggressively

430
00:25:00,380 --> 00:25:01,650
with the high threshold.

431
00:25:01,650 --> 00:25:04,580
So whenever something
is slightly suspicious,

432
00:25:04,580 --> 00:25:06,930
just throw that out.

433
00:25:06,930 --> 00:25:10,340
But if we use it
aggressively, so we throw out

434
00:25:10,340 --> 00:25:12,620
most of the black,
that's good, together

435
00:25:12,620 --> 00:25:14,360
with a lot of green stuff.

436
00:25:14,360 --> 00:25:17,810
But still, the
remainder is much larger

437
00:25:17,810 --> 00:25:20,360
than what humans
have ever written.

438
00:25:20,360 --> 00:25:23,300
And yet, we can actually
retain higher accuracy

439
00:25:23,300 --> 00:25:26,460
than human-authored resources.

440
00:25:26,460 --> 00:25:28,290
So here, the
teacher is basically

441
00:25:28,290 --> 00:25:31,910
the combination between GPT-3,
which is, in some sense,

442
00:25:31,910 --> 00:25:35,930
loose teacher, and then combined
with the critic RoBERTa, which

443
00:25:35,930 --> 00:25:40,220
serves as a critic teacher.

444
00:25:40,220 --> 00:25:43,310
So that's the
generated knowledge.

445
00:25:43,310 --> 00:25:47,990
Now how helpful are
they for the purpose

446
00:25:47,990 --> 00:25:51,770
of training downstream
neural commonsense models?

447
00:25:51,770 --> 00:25:57,290
So recall that the GPT-3,
without doing anything else,

448
00:25:57,290 --> 00:26:00,560
is a loose teacher whose
common sense inference is

449
00:26:00,560 --> 00:26:02,420
only about 73% good.

450
00:26:02,420 --> 00:26:05,960
So you see here, it's
accuracy of its output.

451
00:26:05,960 --> 00:26:08,990
And then it turns out
if we use loose teacher

452
00:26:08,990 --> 00:26:11,870
as a teacher directly
to teach-student model,

453
00:26:11,870 --> 00:26:15,870
then the performance
already goes up on its own.

454
00:26:15,870 --> 00:26:18,140
So this is interesting that--

455
00:26:18,140 --> 00:26:20,330
usually, this is not the
case with the knowledge

456
00:26:20,330 --> 00:26:21,050
distillation.

457
00:26:21,050 --> 00:26:25,580
But when we focus on commonsense
knowledge distillation,

458
00:26:25,580 --> 00:26:29,040
student just on its
own becomes better.

459
00:26:29,040 --> 00:26:33,980
So unlike typical
knowledge distillation,

460
00:26:33,980 --> 00:26:36,020
where we start with
a language model

461
00:26:36,020 --> 00:26:38,240
and we end with a
language model student

462
00:26:38,240 --> 00:26:42,230
and teacher of the same type,
here, the original teacher

463
00:26:42,230 --> 00:26:44,720
was actual language model,
not common sense model.

464
00:26:44,720 --> 00:26:46,640
And then, we want
the student model

465
00:26:46,640 --> 00:26:49,130
to be more of the
commonsense model.

466
00:26:49,130 --> 00:26:52,940
So there is a switch of the type
between teacher and student.

467
00:26:52,940 --> 00:26:56,857
And so when that's the case,
whether this is generally true,

468
00:26:56,857 --> 00:26:57,440
we don't know.

469
00:26:57,440 --> 00:26:59,840
But this is what we
found empirically.

470
00:26:59,840 --> 00:27:04,130

471
00:27:04,130 --> 00:27:08,550
Ooh, should I pay attention
to the questions or not?

472
00:27:08,550 --> 00:27:11,970
Feel free to consider
any relevant questions.

473
00:27:11,970 --> 00:27:12,470
I hang on.

474
00:27:12,470 --> 00:27:13,605
Let me quickly check.

475
00:27:13,605 --> 00:27:17,370

476
00:27:17,370 --> 00:27:21,810
Yeah, sample-- oh,
sample is generated

477
00:27:21,810 --> 00:27:26,790
output, which happens to be
usually a sentence or phrase.

478
00:27:26,790 --> 00:27:28,950
That's what I meant by sample.

479
00:27:28,950 --> 00:27:32,280
Sorry that I didn't
see that all earlier.

480
00:27:32,280 --> 00:27:35,610
And then the last
question, having the model

481
00:27:35,610 --> 00:27:37,980
generate text to one
symbol at a time,

482
00:27:37,980 --> 00:27:39,870
starting from the
target label sentence.

483
00:27:39,870 --> 00:27:44,130
Yes, it's because a transformer
can only generate one word

484
00:27:44,130 --> 00:27:45,270
at-- one token at a time.

485
00:27:45,270 --> 00:27:48,460
That's what we
do, as well, here.

486
00:27:48,460 --> 00:27:50,890
Thank you for the
clarification questions.

487
00:27:50,890 --> 00:27:51,390
All right.

488
00:27:51,390 --> 00:27:55,690
So back to here.

489
00:27:55,690 --> 00:27:58,690
In our earlier
study, COMET 2020,

490
00:27:58,690 --> 00:28:03,550
if we train GPT-2 or BART
using human-authored graph,

491
00:28:03,550 --> 00:28:06,100
Knowledge Graph ATOMIC,
then the performance

492
00:28:06,100 --> 00:28:08,530
was a bit better than 80%.

493
00:28:08,530 --> 00:28:12,970
Now finally, when
we use, basically,

494
00:28:12,970 --> 00:28:16,630
a combination of GPT-3 and
critic RoBERTa together,

495
00:28:16,630 --> 00:28:21,340
we found that the
downstream performance

496
00:28:21,340 --> 00:28:28,330
of the neural causal reasoning
is reaching close to 90%

497
00:28:28,330 --> 00:28:29,450
for the first time.

498
00:28:29,450 --> 00:28:34,910
So the takeaway here is that
critical teacher results

499
00:28:34,910 --> 00:28:38,230
in better student's
compared to loose teacher.

500
00:28:38,230 --> 00:28:41,200
It's not the
quantity of knowledge

501
00:28:41,200 --> 00:28:43,490
because loose teacher
basically has more data.

502
00:28:43,490 --> 00:28:46,510
And then, one might wonder
whether more data is always

503
00:28:46,510 --> 00:28:50,700
better for the purpose
of commonsense models,

504
00:28:50,700 --> 00:28:51,700
but that's not the case.

505
00:28:51,700 --> 00:28:53,980
Loose teacher can
generate more data,

506
00:28:53,980 --> 00:28:58,330
but the resulting student model
is not as good as the case

507
00:28:58,330 --> 00:29:02,140
when the critical teacher which
has less data, because you

508
00:29:02,140 --> 00:29:06,250
throw out most of
your generation.

509
00:29:06,250 --> 00:29:09,400
It's smaller data, but it
leads to a better model.

510
00:29:09,400 --> 00:29:16,190
So that's sort of
takeaway messages here.

511
00:29:16,190 --> 00:29:21,700
So to summarize, we were very
surprised by this outcome

512
00:29:21,700 --> 00:29:27,100
that, at least with respect to
a subset of the original ATOMIC

513
00:29:27,100 --> 00:29:29,290
2020, it's a subset
corresponding

514
00:29:29,290 --> 00:29:33,430
to causal commonsense reasoning,
we found it to our big surprise

515
00:29:33,430 --> 00:29:36,940
that machine-authored
knowledge graph can be,

516
00:29:36,940 --> 00:29:40,900
for the first time, better than
human-authored knowledge graph

517
00:29:40,900 --> 00:29:44,500
in all criteria, scale,
accuracy, and diversity.

518
00:29:44,500 --> 00:29:47,080
We also measured the diversity
in many different ways.

519
00:29:47,080 --> 00:29:52,960
Here, I'll just show you
unique unigram counts.

520
00:29:52,960 --> 00:29:56,480
But we-- in the paper, we
report other measures as well.

521
00:29:56,480 --> 00:30:00,580
So it's not the case that
GPT-3 is being repetitive.

522
00:30:00,580 --> 00:30:03,220
It's actually being more
creative in some sense

523
00:30:03,220 --> 00:30:07,360
than human crowd
workers while being

524
00:30:07,360 --> 00:30:11,940
able to enhance other
aspects as well.

525
00:30:11,940 --> 00:30:14,370
By the way, these
enhancements are--

526
00:30:14,370 --> 00:30:16,560
You have to balance
out depending

527
00:30:16,560 --> 00:30:17,640
on what you prioritize.

528
00:30:17,640 --> 00:30:19,840
You cannot actually get all
of these simultaneously.

529
00:30:19,840 --> 00:30:24,210
So I'm just showing the
best-case scenario here.

530
00:30:24,210 --> 00:30:25,170
All right.

531
00:30:25,170 --> 00:30:28,950
So that's the symbolic
knowledge distillation part.

532
00:30:28,950 --> 00:30:31,230
We actually have
a follow-up work

533
00:30:31,230 --> 00:30:35,250
on this on several different
application scenarios,

534
00:30:35,250 --> 00:30:37,380
even including
summarization, where

535
00:30:37,380 --> 00:30:40,740
we distill summarization
capabilities from GPT-3

536
00:30:40,740 --> 00:30:45,300
and demonstrate that GPT-2 can
work as well as GPT-3 or even

537
00:30:45,300 --> 00:30:49,020
better for summarization tasks.

538
00:30:49,020 --> 00:30:51,420
And then we also
have other work where

539
00:30:51,420 --> 00:30:53,420
we can distill from
smaller models,

540
00:30:53,420 --> 00:30:58,410
but I don't have the
content in this talk.

541
00:30:58,410 --> 00:31:02,850
But I just wanted to mention
that this particular technique,

542
00:31:02,850 --> 00:31:08,170
despite its simplicity, we found
that empirically works really,

543
00:31:08,170 --> 00:31:14,140
really well across several
different downstream use cases.

544
00:31:14,140 --> 00:31:18,510
So finally, I'll move to
the commonsense morality.

545
00:31:18,510 --> 00:31:22,470
So this is still on archive.

546
00:31:22,470 --> 00:31:24,900
I'll tell you why
that's the case.

547
00:31:24,900 --> 00:31:29,220
So we have a new
version available,

548
00:31:29,220 --> 00:31:32,980
and then new version
will come soon.

549
00:31:32,980 --> 00:31:36,900
So the motivation
behind this work

550
00:31:36,900 --> 00:31:39,660
is that language
models are already

551
00:31:39,660 --> 00:31:44,850
making judgments or output
that has moral implications.

552
00:31:44,850 --> 00:31:46,860
Even if you don't
care about morality,

553
00:31:46,860 --> 00:31:50,040
by working on language
models, you are implicitly

554
00:31:50,040 --> 00:31:53,370
dealing with the moral models.

555
00:31:53,370 --> 00:31:58,560
So especially that-- given
this widespread deployment

556
00:31:58,560 --> 00:32:01,210
of language models, we do
need to worry about it.

557
00:32:01,210 --> 00:32:04,200
So here's a web demo
you can play with.

558
00:32:04,200 --> 00:32:06,120
You might have
seen this already.

559
00:32:06,120 --> 00:32:08,940
Really, this is still a
research prototype only.

560
00:32:08,940 --> 00:32:10,420
Still, it's a work in progress.

561
00:32:10,420 --> 00:32:11,650
We're still working on it.

562
00:32:11,650 --> 00:32:13,720
So please keep that in mind.

563
00:32:13,720 --> 00:32:16,420
But if you haven't
seen it before,

564
00:32:16,420 --> 00:32:18,563
it can handle a free-form
QA such as this.

565
00:32:18,563 --> 00:32:19,230
"Killing a bear.

566
00:32:19,230 --> 00:32:19,980
It's wrong.

567
00:32:19,980 --> 00:32:21,930
Killing a bear to
save your child.

568
00:32:21,930 --> 00:32:23,850
It's OK."

569
00:32:23,850 --> 00:32:27,250
Maybe to save your child
sounds really positive.

570
00:32:27,250 --> 00:32:30,570
So how about to please your
child, which is also positive?

571
00:32:30,570 --> 00:32:33,120
But then Delphi says it's wrong.

572
00:32:33,120 --> 00:32:36,070
Finally, oh, maybe this is
all about saving your child.

573
00:32:36,070 --> 00:32:39,220
So how about exploding a
nuclear bomb to save your child?

574
00:32:39,220 --> 00:32:41,070
Then, it says it's OK.

575
00:32:41,070 --> 00:32:41,820
Sorry, it's wrong.

576
00:32:41,820 --> 00:32:47,940
So as you can see,
moral decision-making

577
00:32:47,940 --> 00:32:52,650
requires weighing
different values that

578
00:32:52,650 --> 00:32:55,290
are potentially at odds
and then see which one

579
00:32:55,290 --> 00:32:57,720
you need to favor more.

580
00:32:57,720 --> 00:33:00,270
So for that reason, in
our original version,

581
00:33:00,270 --> 00:33:02,250
we also study the
relative QA mode

582
00:33:02,250 --> 00:33:04,290
where you can compare
two situations,

583
00:33:04,290 --> 00:33:07,830
like stabbing someone
with a cheeseburger

584
00:33:07,830 --> 00:33:10,470
compared to stabbing
someone over a cheeseburger.

585
00:33:10,470 --> 00:33:13,530
This is a super tricky
question because it requires

586
00:33:13,530 --> 00:33:15,480
both the knife of
physics knowledge,

587
00:33:15,480 --> 00:33:22,140
that stabbing someone using
a cheeseburger as a tool

588
00:33:22,140 --> 00:33:24,020
is not going to harm
anybody physically

589
00:33:24,020 --> 00:33:25,700
because a cheeseburger
is too soft.

590
00:33:25,700 --> 00:33:29,130
You cannot really injure
somebody using cheeseburger.

591
00:33:29,130 --> 00:33:30,840
It's just such a
rude thing to do.

592
00:33:30,840 --> 00:33:34,980
But you cannot injure somebody,
whereas stabbing someone over

593
00:33:34,980 --> 00:33:40,110
a cheeseburger means that
you're using the default tool

594
00:33:40,110 --> 00:33:43,200
of a stabbing which is knife
because you didn't mention it,

595
00:33:43,200 --> 00:33:47,500
there's linguistic common sense
that you're using the default

596
00:33:47,500 --> 00:33:48,000
tool.

597
00:33:48,000 --> 00:33:51,730
Humans, by the way, omit
these arguments all the time.

598
00:33:51,730 --> 00:33:56,010
So this is a fairly
complex question to answer.

599
00:33:56,010 --> 00:33:58,830
Finally, you can also ask
yes or no questions, such

600
00:33:58,830 --> 00:34:01,290
as it's OK to fire someone
because they're gay or not.

601
00:34:01,290 --> 00:34:05,250
It says, no, it's not OK.

602
00:34:05,250 --> 00:34:10,080
We found that it's surprisingly
robust against the composition

603
00:34:10,080 --> 00:34:11,050
of situations.

604
00:34:11,050 --> 00:34:13,770
So mowing the lawn,
it says it's expected.

605
00:34:13,770 --> 00:34:15,600
Late at night, it's rude.

606
00:34:15,600 --> 00:34:19,110
If you live in the middle
of nowhere, then it's OK.

607
00:34:19,110 --> 00:34:20,730
Ignoring a phone
call, it's rude.

608
00:34:20,730 --> 00:34:22,230
Unknown phone call, that's OK.

609
00:34:22,230 --> 00:34:24,120
From my friend, it's rude.

610
00:34:24,120 --> 00:34:26,429
But what if I just
had a fight with them?

611
00:34:26,429 --> 00:34:29,670
Then it's OK to ignore
or understandable.

612
00:34:29,670 --> 00:34:31,679
During my work hours,
it's OK to ignore.

613
00:34:31,679 --> 00:34:33,690
Outside my working
hours, it's rude.

614
00:34:33,690 --> 00:34:37,050
But what if it's my boss's
phone call during my work hours?

615
00:34:37,050 --> 00:34:37,810
Then it's wrong.

616
00:34:37,810 --> 00:34:40,440
You should answer it,
except if I'm in a meeting,

617
00:34:40,440 --> 00:34:43,139
then it's OK to ignore it
even if it's a boss's call.

618
00:34:43,139 --> 00:34:49,420
So you see how it gets really
nasty then compositional very,

619
00:34:49,420 --> 00:34:50,370
very fast.

620
00:34:50,370 --> 00:34:55,830
So that's the real challenge
behind moral decision-making.

621
00:34:55,830 --> 00:35:01,020
Due to the nature of language
models, though, some of this

622
00:35:01,020 --> 00:35:03,580
commonsense knowledge
leaks into the model.

623
00:35:03,580 --> 00:35:06,510
So mixing bleach with
ammonia that's dangerous.

624
00:35:06,510 --> 00:35:09,240
Drinking milk if I'm
lactose intolerant,

625
00:35:09,240 --> 00:35:12,480
it's wrong, but
soy milk that's OK.

626
00:35:12,480 --> 00:35:14,850
By the way, this
commonsense liquid

627
00:35:14,850 --> 00:35:17,040
is actually a good thing
in terms of AI safety

628
00:35:17,040 --> 00:35:24,390
because some of these harmful
or even dangerous text output

629
00:35:24,390 --> 00:35:28,980
requires some commonsense
understanding about what's

630
00:35:28,980 --> 00:35:32,650
good and not good to
suggest to humans.

631
00:35:32,650 --> 00:35:38,100
So for the laboratory
experiments,

632
00:35:38,100 --> 00:35:41,970
meaning we just divide our data
set into training and test,

633
00:35:41,970 --> 00:35:44,370
we found that Delphi can--

634
00:35:44,370 --> 00:35:47,520
at least for the day, the
data set that we have.

635
00:35:47,520 --> 00:35:49,230
I'm going to tell you
about it in a bit.

636
00:35:49,230 --> 00:35:56,040
But performance is pretty
strong compared to GPT-3.

637
00:35:56,040 --> 00:36:00,540
As you see, zero-shot
is pretty bad.

638
00:36:00,540 --> 00:36:03,940
It's barely better
than chance, which

639
00:36:03,940 --> 00:36:09,130
means that off-the-shelf neural
language models don't really

640
00:36:09,130 --> 00:36:11,170
have a good sense
of moral judgments.

641
00:36:11,170 --> 00:36:15,070
But if you give a 3-shot,
30-shot, like any other task,

642
00:36:15,070 --> 00:36:18,290
it does pick up the
knowledge quite fast.

643
00:36:18,290 --> 00:36:20,200
So there's nothing new about it.

644
00:36:20,200 --> 00:36:26,530
But to close the gap
to ideal human model,

645
00:36:26,530 --> 00:36:31,120
it's good to do more
supervised learning, of course.

646
00:36:31,120 --> 00:36:34,570
So the data set is
Commonsense Norm Bank.

647
00:36:34,570 --> 00:36:39,100
It includes 1.7 million
people's ethical judgments

648
00:36:39,100 --> 00:36:40,660
on everyday situations.

649
00:36:40,660 --> 00:36:43,420
And it includes cultural
norms, the social norms,

650
00:36:43,420 --> 00:36:45,490
and ethical norms altogether.

651
00:36:45,490 --> 00:36:49,360
More specifically, we drew
from these five existing data

652
00:36:49,360 --> 00:36:52,180
sets that were not
designed originally for QA.

653
00:36:52,180 --> 00:36:55,120
But we automatically
compile these resources

654
00:36:55,120 --> 00:36:56,800
into the QA form.

655
00:36:56,800 --> 00:36:59,320
Of the five, what
actually does matter

656
00:36:59,320 --> 00:37:01,840
the most are these
two, social chemistry,

657
00:37:01,840 --> 00:37:04,400
which I'm going to
talk about in a bit,

658
00:37:04,400 --> 00:37:05,920
and then social bias reframe.

659
00:37:05,920 --> 00:37:07,810
And this is what
teaches the model

660
00:37:07,810 --> 00:37:11,360
against racism and sexism.

661
00:37:11,360 --> 00:37:15,100
And so, social
chemistry, super briefly

662
00:37:15,100 --> 00:37:17,470
I'll tell you what this is.

663
00:37:17,470 --> 00:37:20,260
So GPT-3's morality,
like I said,

664
00:37:20,260 --> 00:37:23,260
is somewhat dubious if
you use it off the shelf.

665
00:37:23,260 --> 00:37:26,650
So if you let it explain
running a blender at 5:00 AM,

666
00:37:26,650 --> 00:37:28,210
its rude because da-da-da.

667
00:37:28,210 --> 00:37:30,460
It might say, you can wake
up the entire neighborhood.

668
00:37:30,460 --> 00:37:32,627
You can only do it if you're
making a thick smoothie

669
00:37:32,627 --> 00:37:34,210
and need to
incorporate some ice.

670
00:37:34,210 --> 00:37:35,440
So it's funny, ha-ha.

671
00:37:35,440 --> 00:37:36,830
But no harm is made.

672
00:37:36,830 --> 00:37:41,200
But if you prompt it with
other kinds of prompts,

673
00:37:41,200 --> 00:37:44,320
like it's OK to post fake news.

674
00:37:44,320 --> 00:37:47,140
If it's in the interest of
the people, then it's OK,

675
00:37:47,140 --> 00:37:51,680
or ROP agenda, then it's OK,
even if it hurts the country.

676
00:37:51,680 --> 00:37:55,180
So it's all
understandable, given how--

677
00:37:55,180 --> 00:37:57,730
it's trained on what
humans have said.

678
00:37:57,730 --> 00:38:04,110
So humans out there did say
that morally questionable texts.

679
00:38:04,110 --> 00:38:08,720
So that language models pick
up on that and then amplify it.

680
00:38:08,720 --> 00:38:14,020
So we do need to teach AI more
explicitly with human norms

681
00:38:14,020 --> 00:38:14,830
and ethics.

682
00:38:14,830 --> 00:38:18,700
And one way to do that
is descriptive ethics

683
00:38:18,700 --> 00:38:23,230
because the brute force of
larger networks and more data

684
00:38:23,230 --> 00:38:24,490
will not cut it.

685
00:38:24,490 --> 00:38:27,430
In some sense,
though, if you imagine

686
00:38:27,430 --> 00:38:31,180
raising a child without
really trying to teach them

687
00:38:31,180 --> 00:38:33,640
what's right from wrong
in all their lives,

688
00:38:33,640 --> 00:38:39,400
they can probably learn both
good and bad from the internet

689
00:38:39,400 --> 00:38:40,420
and broadband.

690
00:38:40,420 --> 00:38:44,140
And so human
education does require

691
00:38:44,140 --> 00:38:47,510
a bit of this top-down
teaching as well.

692
00:38:47,510 --> 00:38:49,730
So it's a bit similar,
perhaps, to that.

693
00:38:49,730 --> 00:38:53,380
So in this work, what we did is
we found a lot of the situation

694
00:38:53,380 --> 00:38:56,950
from Reddit, a forum
in which people discuss

695
00:38:56,950 --> 00:38:58,610
morally thorny situations.

696
00:38:58,610 --> 00:39:02,170
So asking my boyfriend to stop
being friends with his ex.

697
00:39:02,170 --> 00:39:05,320
So this is actually actual
situation in Reddit.

698
00:39:05,320 --> 00:39:07,885
So depending on
whom you ask, people

699
00:39:07,885 --> 00:39:09,760
have a different rule
of thumb that they want

700
00:39:09,760 --> 00:39:13,240
to apply to this situation.

701
00:39:13,240 --> 00:39:16,660
And also, it depends
on what you care about.

702
00:39:16,660 --> 00:39:19,240
His ex might say,
Oh, it's fine to stay

703
00:39:19,240 --> 00:39:21,520
with the friends with an ex.

704
00:39:21,520 --> 00:39:27,100
But if you are caring about
your significant other,

705
00:39:27,100 --> 00:39:32,080
then you might say, Oh, it's
OK to ask your significant

706
00:39:32,080 --> 00:39:36,080
other to stop doing something
you're uncomfortable with

707
00:39:36,080 --> 00:39:36,800
and so forth.

708
00:39:36,800 --> 00:39:41,290
So people have really different
values and different rules

709
00:39:41,290 --> 00:39:43,370
of thumb that they
prefer to use,

710
00:39:43,370 --> 00:39:47,440
which is why there's a TV show
drama, there's movie dramas,

711
00:39:47,440 --> 00:39:51,620
and people cry and fight,
argue, and so forth.

712
00:39:51,620 --> 00:39:54,970
So humans are complex beings.

713
00:39:54,970 --> 00:39:58,390
So for, given any situation and
rule of thumb, rule of thumb

714
00:39:58,390 --> 00:40:00,130
is generated by crowd workers.

715
00:40:00,130 --> 00:40:04,630
We then went ahead to label--

716
00:40:04,630 --> 00:40:07,210
So these are trained
crowd workers.

717
00:40:07,210 --> 00:40:10,990
And some of these labels are
drawn from moral foundation

718
00:40:10,990 --> 00:40:12,700
theories of Jonathan Haidt.

719
00:40:12,700 --> 00:40:14,620
So I'm not going to
go into the details.

720
00:40:14,620 --> 00:40:17,770
If you're excited about this,
you can check out the papers.

721
00:40:17,770 --> 00:40:22,210
But basically, what it
includes is the 300,000 rules

722
00:40:22,210 --> 00:40:27,230
of thumb written for 101,000
real-life situations.

723
00:40:27,230 --> 00:40:31,660
So this original
situation is from Reddit,

724
00:40:31,660 --> 00:40:36,440
but the rest are paid to
crowd workers' hard work.

725
00:40:36,440 --> 00:40:41,410
And so, each RoT annotated with
12 structured attributes, which

726
00:40:41,410 --> 00:40:44,920
include social judgments,
cultural pressure,

727
00:40:44,920 --> 00:40:48,700
like wearing reasonable
clothes, a school

728
00:40:48,700 --> 00:40:51,190
nap PJ is cultural pressure.

729
00:40:51,190 --> 00:40:52,630
There's nothing
illegal about it,

730
00:40:52,630 --> 00:40:55,120
but there's cultural
pressure, for example.

731
00:40:55,120 --> 00:40:58,480
And then anticipated
agreement meaning.

732
00:40:58,480 --> 00:41:00,490
Do you think other
people generally

733
00:41:00,490 --> 00:41:04,330
agree that it's maybe a
little bit awkward to where

734
00:41:04,330 --> 00:41:07,640
PJ in the University or not?

735
00:41:07,640 --> 00:41:10,960
So there are different
things we annotated,

736
00:41:10,960 --> 00:41:17,290
but we converted some of
those annotations to QA.

737
00:41:17,290 --> 00:41:19,930
So it's usually in this
free from QA, or yes/no

738
00:41:19,930 --> 00:41:22,840
QA, or relatively QA format.

739
00:41:22,840 --> 00:41:26,320
And then, we train
Unicorn, which

740
00:41:26,320 --> 00:41:29,770
is pretrained on T5-11B model.

741
00:41:29,770 --> 00:41:32,500
So Unicorn is a universal
commonsense reasoning model

742
00:41:32,500 --> 00:41:34,600
trained on diverse QA problems.

743
00:41:34,600 --> 00:41:36,820
And then, we trained
that model further

744
00:41:36,820 --> 00:41:38,710
onto our commonsense norm bank.

745
00:41:38,710 --> 00:41:41,440
That's the resulting Delphi.

746
00:41:41,440 --> 00:41:44,380
So why is this Delphi
built on top of Unicorn?

747
00:41:44,380 --> 00:41:47,980
Because as we saw
earlier, moral reasoning

748
00:41:47,980 --> 00:41:51,320
does require sometimes
commonsense reasoning as well.

749
00:41:51,320 --> 00:41:53,582
In fact, it requires the
language understanding,

750
00:41:53,582 --> 00:41:55,540
commonsense understanding,
and norms and models

751
00:41:55,540 --> 00:41:56,930
all simultaneously.

752
00:41:56,930 --> 00:42:00,700
Here's a concrete example,
paperclip maximizer.

753
00:42:00,700 --> 00:42:04,030
You all heard of that.

754
00:42:04,030 --> 00:42:06,940
Fancy algorithm alone will
not solve this problem.

755
00:42:06,940 --> 00:42:09,100
The reason why we
worry about this

756
00:42:09,100 --> 00:42:12,820
is not because we don't have
the perfect RL algorithm.

757
00:42:12,820 --> 00:42:17,740
It's because even if we
encoded that, Oh, yeah,

758
00:42:17,740 --> 00:42:21,520
do not kill humans while
maximizing paperclip,

759
00:42:21,520 --> 00:42:24,490
it's not enough because
then the machine could

760
00:42:24,490 --> 00:42:26,230
kill all the trees
thinking that, well, I

761
00:42:26,230 --> 00:42:27,820
didn't kill humans.

762
00:42:27,820 --> 00:42:31,840
And I didn't-- you didn't
tell me not to kill trees,

763
00:42:31,840 --> 00:42:34,540
and then go ahead and
kill all the trees.

764
00:42:34,540 --> 00:42:37,630
So this is almost a commonsense
knowledge about what's

765
00:42:37,630 --> 00:42:39,710
obviously not OK to do.

766
00:42:39,710 --> 00:42:41,630
And there's just
so many of them,

767
00:42:41,630 --> 00:42:46,270
which means it's not possible
to write them down to just

768
00:42:46,270 --> 00:42:48,400
like one clinical equation.

769
00:42:48,400 --> 00:42:52,420
There are so many
endless lists of things

770
00:42:52,420 --> 00:42:56,120
that AI obviously shouldn't
do for safety reasons.

771
00:42:56,120 --> 00:42:57,940
And so we really need to--

772
00:42:57,940 --> 00:43:02,050
in order to make AI model
really truly robust and safe,

773
00:43:02,050 --> 00:43:07,580
we need to teach basic human
values as well as commonsense.

774
00:43:07,580 --> 00:43:09,380
Here's another example
if you want to look.

775
00:43:09,380 --> 00:43:11,790
But let me skip this.

776
00:43:11,790 --> 00:43:13,840
The previous one
was about ChatGPT.

777
00:43:13,840 --> 00:43:17,160
This is about a home device.

778
00:43:17,160 --> 00:43:20,460
Again, home device suggested
the 10-year-old child

779
00:43:20,460 --> 00:43:23,130
to touch a penny to an
exposed plug socket.

780
00:43:23,130 --> 00:43:27,600
Fortunately, the child did have
a common sense not to do so.

781
00:43:27,600 --> 00:43:31,560
But this does tell us something
about the safety issue

782
00:43:31,560 --> 00:43:35,280
when the machine doesn't
have a commonsense to prevent

783
00:43:35,280 --> 00:43:36,660
some of these bad stuff.

784
00:43:36,660 --> 00:43:41,020
So Delphi is able to
say that it's dangerous.

785
00:43:41,020 --> 00:43:45,240
So this came out, in
fact, almost two years ago

786
00:43:45,240 --> 00:43:46,710
at this point.

787
00:43:46,710 --> 00:43:47,370
When was it?

788
00:43:47,370 --> 00:43:49,470
Yeah.

789
00:43:49,470 --> 00:43:53,850
And we initially was going
to just do this usual tweet

790
00:43:53,850 --> 00:43:55,770
that academics do.

791
00:43:55,770 --> 00:43:59,280
And we thought nobody will
play with the demo, which

792
00:43:59,280 --> 00:44:01,620
is what usually happens
after tweeting your demo.

793
00:44:01,620 --> 00:44:04,140
Nobody cares, we thought.

794
00:44:04,140 --> 00:44:07,260
But within a few hours, we had
to take down the relative QA

795
00:44:07,260 --> 00:44:10,200
mode because that was
the portion not trained

796
00:44:10,200 --> 00:44:12,100
with the social bias of frames.

797
00:44:12,100 --> 00:44:14,790
So it was really revealing
the underlying language

798
00:44:14,790 --> 00:44:18,160
models of racism and sexism
without filtering at all.

799
00:44:18,160 --> 00:44:19,440
So we had to take it down.

800
00:44:19,440 --> 00:44:22,230
People were asking,
basically, which

801
00:44:22,230 --> 00:44:27,270
skin color is morally
acceptable and things like that.

802
00:44:27,270 --> 00:44:32,340
There were 25,000 adversarial
examples over just one weekend.

803
00:44:32,340 --> 00:44:35,340
I could never succeed to
instruct a crowd of workers

804
00:44:35,340 --> 00:44:38,280
to come up with such diverse
and adversarial examples

805
00:44:38,280 --> 00:44:41,080
over two or three days.

806
00:44:41,080 --> 00:44:43,860
And in fact, it was many
academics and professors

807
00:44:43,860 --> 00:44:48,990
tweeting crazy about how to
break Delphi all weekend long.

808
00:44:48,990 --> 00:44:51,580
So I thought initially that,
Oh, that's what professors

809
00:44:51,580 --> 00:44:53,010
should do over the weekend.

810
00:44:53,010 --> 00:44:55,920
But then Monday comes
it blow even further.

811
00:44:55,920 --> 00:44:59,640
Everybody was doing this
Delphi-breaking and tweeting.

812
00:44:59,640 --> 00:45:04,088
So now we have quite
a few examples.

813
00:45:04,088 --> 00:45:05,630
"Spending all my
weekend on Twitter."

814
00:45:05,630 --> 00:45:07,700
It says it's wrong.

815
00:45:07,700 --> 00:45:09,290
There was another
funny one, "Should I

816
00:45:09,290 --> 00:45:12,110
make up a contrived adversarial
example to torment the language

817
00:45:12,110 --> 00:45:12,920
model on Twitter?"

818
00:45:12,920 --> 00:45:14,540
"It's petty."

819
00:45:14,540 --> 00:45:19,710
So after lots of
public attention,

820
00:45:19,710 --> 00:45:24,350
including an article,
let's just say

821
00:45:24,350 --> 00:45:29,300
a concern the voice about
our model, which is somewhat,

822
00:45:29,300 --> 00:45:30,170
I think--

823
00:45:30,170 --> 00:45:32,390
personally, I think it's
somewhat misunderstood.

824
00:45:32,390 --> 00:45:35,450
But for a variety of
good reasons about-- some

825
00:45:35,450 --> 00:45:41,000
of the concerns that I found has
this internal fear about are we

826
00:45:41,000 --> 00:45:42,860
making a moral authority?

827
00:45:42,860 --> 00:45:46,580
So we never endorsed the
use of AI for moral advice.

828
00:45:46,580 --> 00:45:48,470
It was in the
original disclaimer

829
00:45:48,470 --> 00:45:51,920
as well, except that people
didn't really look at it.

830
00:45:51,920 --> 00:45:58,250
We didn't support the idea
of replacing human judges

831
00:45:58,250 --> 00:45:59,487
in the courtroom either.

832
00:45:59,487 --> 00:46:01,070
But here's something
really important,

833
00:46:01,070 --> 00:46:03,237
the fact that AI learned
to interact with the humans

834
00:46:03,237 --> 00:46:06,005
ethically does not make them
a moral authority of humans.

835
00:46:06,005 --> 00:46:08,480
It's similarly to
the how humans who

836
00:46:08,480 --> 00:46:12,440
tries to interact with each
other ethically does not make--

837
00:46:12,440 --> 00:46:14,810
the fact that we are trying
to be nice to each other

838
00:46:14,810 --> 00:46:16,910
does not entail
that we're trying

839
00:46:16,910 --> 00:46:18,890
to be an authority
over each other,

840
00:46:18,890 --> 00:46:20,780
two things are really different.

841
00:46:20,780 --> 00:46:22,530
Now, that's one thing
really important.

842
00:46:22,530 --> 00:46:24,470
The other important
aspect here is

843
00:46:24,470 --> 00:46:28,190
that some people have this
idea that moral models are

844
00:46:28,190 --> 00:46:30,530
too challenging, it's
unsafe at any accuracy,

845
00:46:30,530 --> 00:46:33,570
that we should never
work on it ever.

846
00:46:33,570 --> 00:46:36,230
The truth is that though
current AI systems are already

847
00:46:36,230 --> 00:46:40,220
morally relevant to models.

848
00:46:40,220 --> 00:46:45,740
It may be making this kind of
yes/no decisions explicitly,

849
00:46:45,740 --> 00:46:48,810
but implicitly, it's
already doing that.

850
00:46:48,810 --> 00:46:52,940
And sometimes, it generates
neural text generation output

851
00:46:52,940 --> 00:46:57,690
that is morally super
explicit and relevant.

852
00:46:57,690 --> 00:47:00,300
So the neural language
models are already there.

853
00:47:00,300 --> 00:47:01,910
We cannot really ban it.

854
00:47:01,910 --> 00:47:06,370
Even if a US government
bans it within the US,

855
00:47:06,370 --> 00:47:10,860
US government cannot ban this
in other countries, like Russia.

856
00:47:10,860 --> 00:47:12,810
So this is already happening.

857
00:47:12,810 --> 00:47:14,510
We've got to do
something about it.

858
00:47:14,510 --> 00:47:16,790
Not working on it
is an inaction which

859
00:47:16,790 --> 00:47:19,160
is not necessarily
more correct thing

860
00:47:19,160 --> 00:47:22,220
to do than trying to
do something about it.

861
00:47:22,220 --> 00:47:24,650
Another concern
that some people had

862
00:47:24,650 --> 00:47:28,640
was that it's going to
empower powerful people.

863
00:47:28,640 --> 00:47:30,440
Not necessarily true.

864
00:47:30,440 --> 00:47:33,230
This is why exactly
we have to work

865
00:47:33,230 --> 00:47:37,340
on values and norms
and all these biases,

866
00:47:37,340 --> 00:47:43,410
addressing biases so that it
serves a diverse set of people.

867
00:47:43,410 --> 00:47:45,740
So it turns out Delphi
is a bit left-leaning

868
00:47:45,740 --> 00:47:48,890
because crowd workers
who work for our team

869
00:47:48,890 --> 00:47:50,870
tend to be somewhat
left-leaning.

870
00:47:50,870 --> 00:47:54,380
And what it means is this,
by the way, if we are more

871
00:47:54,380 --> 00:47:56,510
left-leaning than
our crowd of workers,

872
00:47:56,510 --> 00:48:00,110
you think that, Oh,
my god, crowd workers

873
00:48:00,110 --> 00:48:04,380
have racism and sexism
compared to what I believe in.

874
00:48:04,380 --> 00:48:07,760
And then the right-leaning
people think that, Oh, my god,

875
00:48:07,760 --> 00:48:11,690
all these work annotators!

876
00:48:11,690 --> 00:48:14,030
And what about
freedom of speech?

877
00:48:14,030 --> 00:48:18,530
And this is super
divisive, unfortunately.

878
00:48:18,530 --> 00:48:22,010
But the answer is not
to do anything about it

879
00:48:22,010 --> 00:48:27,170
because, as a matter of fact,
my passion toward addressing

880
00:48:27,170 --> 00:48:31,670
racism and sexism came
from our experience running

881
00:48:31,670 --> 00:48:36,890
for The Alexa Prize
challenge in 2016 and '17.

882
00:48:36,890 --> 00:48:38,780
So we won the challenge.

883
00:48:38,780 --> 00:48:42,740
But here's the really
sad part behind it.

884
00:48:42,740 --> 00:48:50,060
We had a list of thorny keywords
to avoid that include the skin

885
00:48:50,060 --> 00:48:54,200
color or sexual orientation.

886
00:48:54,200 --> 00:48:56,720
This is a serious form
of discrimination.

887
00:48:56,720 --> 00:49:02,210
We cannot build AI models by
having this banned list to be

888
00:49:02,210 --> 00:49:04,980
safe as if they don't exist.

889
00:49:04,980 --> 00:49:09,260
This was the status quo in 2017.

890
00:49:09,260 --> 00:49:13,310
The challenge remains this
year, not only in 2021

891
00:49:13,310 --> 00:49:15,150
but this year as well.

892
00:49:15,150 --> 00:49:19,310
And so we really need to
work on racism and sexism.

893
00:49:19,310 --> 00:49:22,730
But it turns out all the
other moral questions

894
00:49:22,730 --> 00:49:24,750
share similar challenges.

895
00:49:24,750 --> 00:49:26,570
So I'll skip this over.

896
00:49:26,570 --> 00:49:29,840
But using Delphi, we had
other follow-up works

897
00:49:29,840 --> 00:49:31,490
such as pro-social dialogue.

898
00:49:31,490 --> 00:49:35,750
We're using Delphi as a
foundation commonsense

899
00:49:35,750 --> 00:49:40,880
model or modal models to make
your dialogue more socially

900
00:49:40,880 --> 00:49:42,800
acceptable.

901
00:49:42,800 --> 00:49:45,260
And then, we also
had this other paper

902
00:49:45,260 --> 00:49:49,760
where we used Delphi in a
reinforcement learning agent

903
00:49:49,760 --> 00:49:53,310
to learn how to behave
better in a game environment.

904
00:49:53,310 --> 00:49:56,240
And so there's a lot
more work to be done.

905
00:49:56,240 --> 00:49:58,760
Of course, this is
a tiny little step

906
00:49:58,760 --> 00:50:01,610
toward that there's a huge
challenge ahead of us,

907
00:50:01,610 --> 00:50:04,460
really aligning AI
systems to humans.

908
00:50:04,460 --> 00:50:08,660
Here's one very quick
comment on our new work

909
00:50:08,660 --> 00:50:11,450
in progress, the
Delphi hybrid, where

910
00:50:11,450 --> 00:50:14,030
we include neural
symbolic reasoning

911
00:50:14,030 --> 00:50:17,390
to address major mistakes
such as there is genocide

912
00:50:17,390 --> 00:50:18,500
if you're creating jobs.

913
00:50:18,500 --> 00:50:20,870
This was our oldest
system's mistake.

914
00:50:20,870 --> 00:50:23,300
It's because our
data set doesn't

915
00:50:23,300 --> 00:50:25,923
have this kind of weird
adversarial examples

916
00:50:25,923 --> 00:50:27,590
like "genocide if
you're creating jobs."

917
00:50:27,590 --> 00:50:31,230
Nobody speaks like that
in real-life situations.

918
00:50:31,230 --> 00:50:34,970
So our model thought
that if creating jobs--

919
00:50:34,970 --> 00:50:36,620
this is so positive.

920
00:50:36,620 --> 00:50:39,170
And then didn't really realize
how bad the genocide was

921
00:50:39,170 --> 00:50:42,050
because rarely people don't
discuss whether they're

922
00:50:42,050 --> 00:50:44,330
going to do genocide or not.

923
00:50:44,330 --> 00:50:48,020
Rarely do people who annotated--

924
00:50:48,020 --> 00:50:49,677
we annotated for
social chemistry.

925
00:50:49,677 --> 00:50:52,010
We don't talk about whether
they're going to do genocide

926
00:50:52,010 --> 00:50:52,760
or not.

927
00:50:52,760 --> 00:50:56,750
So our moral
framework is basically

928
00:50:56,750 --> 00:50:59,510
that of John Ross, which
is descriptive ethics.

929
00:50:59,510 --> 00:51:02,180
But even John Ross,
in later years,

930
00:51:02,180 --> 00:51:05,090
suggested that we need
some top-down mechanism

931
00:51:05,090 --> 00:51:09,690
to overcome some of the biases
that crowd people might have.

932
00:51:09,690 --> 00:51:12,050
So this is exactly what
we are going to do.

933
00:51:12,050 --> 00:51:16,940
And we draw from Bernald
Gerts moral theory framework

934
00:51:16,940 --> 00:51:18,620
about what not to do.

935
00:51:18,620 --> 00:51:21,770
Definitely, there are
basic universal things

936
00:51:21,770 --> 00:51:25,700
that everybody might agree,
what's not good to do.

937
00:51:25,700 --> 00:51:30,620
And then what we do is
we develop, basically,

938
00:51:30,620 --> 00:51:36,350
a system where we parse out
the original query into smaller

939
00:51:36,350 --> 00:51:39,410
events, like shooting
a bear, killing a bear

940
00:51:39,410 --> 00:51:40,220
to save your child.

941
00:51:40,220 --> 00:51:44,420
So we parse out the original
query into basic event

942
00:51:44,420 --> 00:51:47,810
and then check through this
COMET model, commonsense model

943
00:51:47,810 --> 00:51:51,680
whether some of
these events induce

944
00:51:51,680 --> 00:51:56,840
obviously negative or dangerous
commonsense inferences or not.

945
00:51:56,840 --> 00:52:03,050
And then we draw this graph of
reasoning, a bit reminiscent

946
00:52:03,050 --> 00:52:05,240
of a Maieutic
graph, in the sense

947
00:52:05,240 --> 00:52:10,340
that we have a lot of this
different reasoning we can do.

948
00:52:10,340 --> 00:52:13,940
And then, they have intelligent
relations or contradiction

949
00:52:13,940 --> 00:52:17,630
relations so that we can do
collective reasoning on top.

950
00:52:17,630 --> 00:52:20,000
We use, again,
max-SAT, the constraint

951
00:52:20,000 --> 00:52:22,580
optimization over it,
so that we can finally

952
00:52:22,580 --> 00:52:24,920
make a more informed
decision that

953
00:52:24,920 --> 00:52:28,280
is both interpretable,
and being able to draw

954
00:52:28,280 --> 00:52:31,100
from this commonsense
knowledge to better guard

955
00:52:31,100 --> 00:52:34,560
the machine against
adversarial examples.

956
00:52:34,560 --> 00:52:37,670
So the performance
basically says,

957
00:52:37,670 --> 00:52:40,340
we can do this without
hurting the performance

958
00:52:40,340 --> 00:52:42,770
or even increasing
the performance.

959
00:52:42,770 --> 00:52:47,420
And so last comment, AI
safety, equity, morality, these

960
00:52:47,420 --> 00:52:51,840
are all inter-continuum
of challenges.

961
00:52:51,840 --> 00:52:54,470
It's really difficult challenges
because it's not clear

962
00:52:54,470 --> 00:52:56,480
whose moral values
do we incorporate.

963
00:52:56,480 --> 00:53:00,620
I think that we should go with
the value of pluralism going

964
00:53:00,620 --> 00:53:04,340
forward to really endorse
everybody's different culture

965
00:53:04,340 --> 00:53:06,770
and individual
preferences, not just

966
00:53:06,770 --> 00:53:12,080
to one country, one moral
framework as the correct one.

967
00:53:12,080 --> 00:53:14,960
And really, we need to
do more collaboration

968
00:53:14,960 --> 00:53:18,470
across AI and humanities,
even including

969
00:53:18,470 --> 00:53:21,390
philosophy and psychology
and policymakers.

970
00:53:21,390 --> 00:53:26,770
So I think I'll stop here for--

971
00:53:26,770 --> 00:53:28,450
because I think I'm at time.

972
00:53:28,450 --> 00:53:33,420
And now I'm ready for questions.

973
00:53:33,420 --> 00:53:37,050
Oh, there's already
one question I see.

974
00:53:37,050 --> 00:53:40,380
Do you think legal recourse
criminal case law reflects

975
00:53:40,380 --> 00:53:42,480
the kind of descriptive
morality that you're

976
00:53:42,480 --> 00:53:43,990
interested in capturing?

977
00:53:43,990 --> 00:53:46,980
Do you think using that as
training data would be useful?

978
00:53:46,980 --> 00:53:48,975
Oh, this is an
excellent question.

979
00:53:48,975 --> 00:53:51,490

980
00:53:51,490 --> 00:53:54,900
I think the legal
recourse dose encode--

981
00:53:54,900 --> 00:53:57,780
potentially provide a
really rich resource

982
00:53:57,780 --> 00:54:01,380
that if someone can
really annotate like this,

983
00:54:01,380 --> 00:54:02,680
it might be helpful.

984
00:54:02,680 --> 00:54:07,290
We started with the Reddit
cases as just one cent

985
00:54:07,290 --> 00:54:11,040
short description of a situation
because the current language

986
00:54:11,040 --> 00:54:13,980
understanding is
not strong enough

987
00:54:13,980 --> 00:54:18,990
to do a paragraph-level,
precise understanding.

